    {
      "id": "f9720b8f74a871cddf26e542f2e783b4a89c3512",
      "individual_note": false,
      "resolvable": true,
      "resolved": true,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 658095557,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "> What is the difference between `ONE_BLOCK_SUPPORTED` and `MULTIPLE_BLOCKS_SUPPORTED` for the WTAP_BLOCK_PACKET?\n\n`ONE_BLOCK_SUPPORTED` means \"the file supports only one block of this type\".  For `WTAP_BLOCK_PACKET`, that's only used for the cases where we're really acting as Fileshark, i.e. where the file really has no packets and we're dissecting the raw file contents.  A packet capture file format that supports only one packet per file isn't very useful.\n\n> I can add the `WTAP_BLOCK_IF_ID_AND_INFO`.\n\nPlease do.\n\n> btw: I cannot make dragging multiple files into Wireshark work for BLF\n\nPresumably you're referring to selecting and dragging multiple files in one operation and having Wireshark merge those files into a single capture.\n\n> since BLF does not have all the interface blocks defined at the start of the file but I need to learn interfaces on the fly.\n\nNeither pcapng nor iptrace nor ERF are guaranteed to have all the interface blocks defined at the start of the file.\n\n> Wireshark seems to not accept that.\n\nIf not, that's presumably not a BLF-specific problem; as noted, there are other file formats with that issue.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-08-23T18:09:56.297Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-08-23T18:22:13.511Z",
